From 7ee316beba34c58e432dcd629de1bb9046ff5fca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:30:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 109/112] Filter crossing guard (Community features, off by
 default): both resonances lowered where the HPF meets the LPF

With both filters on in series and the HPF's cutoff near the LPF's or above it, their resonance peaks stack: the
gains multiply, the peak heights in dB add (up to 24 dB above the higher single peak, tests/filters crossing_guard).
Switched on (Community features > Filter crossing guard, 7-segment CROS, saved in CommunityFeatures.XML as
filterCrossingGuard), FilterSet::setConfig() lowers each filter's resonance to where its own peak is half as high
(+0.5 dB), so together they peak at most 1 dB above the higher one alone: (hL + 1) / 2 + (hH + 1) / 2 <=
max(hL, hH) + 1. The peak heights come from measured tables per mode (12 / 24 dB, drive and HP ladders, SVF) at
resonance 0 to 50; where a ladder sings on its own the guard takes it down to about 23 dB. Strength by the cutoffs'
distance: 0 from 2 octaves below, full from 0.7 times the LPF's cutoff to 1.4 times, 0 at 8 times (smoothsteps).
Monotonic in the knob's resonance at every strength. The coefficients ramp per sample from block to block, so the
lowered resonance moves smoothly with a swept or automated cutoff. Voices and the song's, kits' and audio tracks'
filters alike (the audio engine sets FilterSet::crossingGuard before each render). Not in the parallel route, where
the sum at half level doesn't stack. Off: the branch isn't taken, the filters render bit for bit as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
